# The committed ABI baseline is the trusted "old side" every PR gets
# compared against (abi-scan.yml reads it from the PR's base SHA via
# `git show`, not from the PR's own working tree, specifically so a PR
# can't rewrite its own baseline). Requiring review on it here is the
# second half of that defense: even a baseline edit that *lands on main*
# needs a human to sign off, so `baseline.yml`'s bot-authored refresh
# commits are the only unreviewed path this file can change through.
#
# That protection covers the baseline's *output* -- but baseline.yml runs
# with `contents: write` and can commit/push any abi/math.abicheck.json,
# so a PR could equally bypass review by editing the *producer* instead:
# change what baseline.yml or normalize_baseline.py generates rather than
# editing abi/math.abicheck.json directly (Codex review). Same ownership
# boundary on both, so neither path around the other is unreviewed.
#
# And neither of those matters if the *consumer* of the comparison isn't
# protected either: a PR touching .github/workflows/abi-scan.yml could
# remove or gut the "Enforce gate" step while keeping the `scan` job name
# branch protection requires, so the required check reports success
# without the trusted-baseline comparison ever actually running (Codex
# review). Same boundary here too.
#
# And none of the above holds if this file itself is unprotected: without
# a self-ownership rule, a PR could just delete or weaken the entries
# above (with no owner sign-off) before touching the baseline, its
# producer, or the gating workflow (CodeRabbit review).
#
# This only has teeth once branch protection on `main` requires review
# from code owners for matching paths.
abi/** @napetrov
.github/workflows/baseline.yml @napetrov
.github/workflows/abi-scan.yml @napetrov
scripts/normalize_baseline.py @napetrov
.github/CODEOWNERS @napetrov
